1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,440
Interesting enough, there actually is research on this.

2
00:00:04,440 --> 00:00:08,560
Like so, how many steps down of advanced reasoning

3
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:10,300
that people do, right?

4
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:11,520
Or let's flip it this way.

5
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:15,100
There is a number to assume that will maximize your payoff.

6
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:18,480
Because in general, if you assume infinite,

7
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:20,880
like other people are doing an infinite number of steps,

8
00:00:21,220 --> 00:00:22,600
you will actually do very poorly

9
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:23,920
because your prediction will be way off

10
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:25,740
because normal human beings don't do infinite steps.

11
00:00:26,680 --> 00:00:28,020
On the other hand, if you do no steps,

12
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:29,260
then often you'll generally do poorly

13
00:00:29,260 --> 00:00:30,860
because most people are strategic to some degree.

14
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:33,400
So, at least for some games,

15
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:36,280
people have actually tried to measure how many levels

16
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:41,540
of rationalization should you ascribe to your opponent

17
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:43,760
in order to maximize how well you do?

18
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:45,720
And the answer is something like two or three.

19
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:51,360
So, there's, like if you remember, we'll talk

20
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:52,940
about this after the midterm, but if you remember

21
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:56,280
like the Akerlof-Lemons problem, don't worry about it

22
00:00:56,280 --> 00:00:58,360
if you've never heard about it, but the equilibrium

23
00:00:58,360 --> 00:01:00,740
in the Akerlof-Lemons problem in the standard form is

24
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:04,680
for all cooperation right down, but the actual best strategy

25
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:07,820
to play when you're playing against normal human beings is

26
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:10,220
to figure they'll do two steps of reasoning

27
00:01:10,220 --> 00:01:11,620
and then stop and get lazy.

28
00:01:11,620 --> 00:01:13,780
And then you just do what's optimum there,

29
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:16,000
and then you will actually do better than they will.

30
00:01:17,180 --> 00:01:18,180
Anyway. Okay.

31
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:19,360
All right.

32
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:22,020
So, with mixed strategy and natural equilibrium,

33
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:24,100
you can get through all this complexity

34
00:01:24,100 --> 00:01:26,000
of second guessing, third guessing.

35
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,780
So, you can think, you can interpret the number of layers

36
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:32,400
of thinking you do as another way of randomizing.

37
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:34,840
Since there isn't really any good story

38
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:38,160
for why someone will do one level of thinking, two, three,

39
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:40,040
four, five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten, eleven,

40
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:42,320
a million levels of reasoning.

41
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:45,060
If there isn't any good game theoretic reason for that,

42
00:01:45,620 --> 00:01:48,500
then you can just think of all of this talk about what's going

43
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:50,760
to happen is really about the same as rolling a die.

44
00:01:50,760 --> 00:01:54,720
Okay. Okay.

45
00:01:55,060 --> 00:01:58,080
And, of course, we can go and, as I said,

46
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:01,560
so since if there is under very weak assumptions,

47
00:02:01,560 --> 00:02:04,560
is that there's always a natural equilibrium.

48
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:05,560
Have I told you that?

49
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:06,960
So, under weak assumptions,

50
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:08,160
there's always a natural equilibrium.

51
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:09,040
It will always exist.

52
00:02:09,580 --> 00:02:11,020
And in this game here, we just proved

53
00:02:11,020 --> 00:02:12,760
that there's no pure strategy in natural equilibrium.

54
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:14,860
So, what kind of equilibrium must exist?

55
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:16,340
A mixed strategy.

56
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:17,900
So, there must be a mixed strategy in natural equilibrium

57
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:18,520
for this game.

58
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:22,320
Well, you can go and solve for it doing exactly this thing all

59
00:02:22,320 --> 00:02:26,480
over here, although if you just take a really quick look

60
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:28,920
at these, you may immediately get an intuition

61
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:29,800
for what must happen.

62
00:02:30,740 --> 00:02:31,880
Yeah, one half, one half.

63
00:02:32,380 --> 00:02:33,340
And that's exactly correct.

64
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:35,600
So, since we have all these symmetric payoffs,

65
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:37,900
then the natural equilibrium is going to be,

66
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:41,060
there's a 50% chance that the criminal gets on the subway

67
00:02:41,060 --> 00:02:43,440
and a 50% chance the Popeye's on the subway.

68
00:02:43,940 --> 00:02:47,240
And then, given that probability distribution,

69
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:49,380
neither one wants to do a different probability

70
00:02:49,380 --> 00:02:52,600
distribution because, of course, if either one went

71
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:58,000
up to 51%, then there's a clear thing you should do.

72
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,140
So, if you're the criminal, if the criminal plays 51% on,

73
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:02,260
then what does Popeye want to do?

74
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:05,780
On all the time, actually.

75
00:03:05,780 --> 00:03:08,480
That one, on all the time maximizes his expected payout.

76
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:11,680
And if Popeye is on 51% of the time,

77
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:13,080
what does the criminal want to do?

78
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:14,740
Off all the time.

79
00:03:14,740 --> 00:03:19,960
So, it's very clear why any deviation

80
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:24,920
from 50-50 is going to get you, is not going to be stable.

81
00:03:25,320 --> 00:03:28,440
So, whereas if it is exactly 50-50, then it is stable.

82
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:30,920
Or, you know, that is an equilibrium.

83
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:35,680
And, by the way, so I'm not sure, but at least

84
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:38,840
in some earlier years, I knew some Austrian economists here

85
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:41,140
like talking about the Holmes-Moriarty problem.

86
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:42,160
You've heard of this?

87
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:43,640
So, this is Sherlock Holmes

88
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:46,120
and his archenemy Moriarty.

89
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:50,100
And Holmes wants to be in the same city as Moriarty,

90
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:52,060
and Moriarty wants to be in a different city than Holmes.

91
00:03:52,520 --> 00:03:54,280
And as you might notice, it's exactly the same thing.

92
00:03:55,100 --> 00:03:58,160
Right? And again, so in the actual books,

93
00:03:58,160 --> 00:04:00,280
I think that Holmes says, well, I deduce

94
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:01,600
that you would definitely be here.

95
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:04,680
Right? And he gives some ridiculous argument

96
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:05,600
that makes no sense.

97
00:04:06,100 --> 00:04:09,580
And, you know, again, it sounds logical because I reason

98
00:04:09,580 --> 00:04:11,440
that you would reason that I would be here,

99
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:12,820
but therefore you wouldn't want to be here,

100
00:04:12,820 --> 00:04:14,540
and therefore you went the other place, but I went there.

101
00:04:14,540 --> 00:04:17,360
Ha! But Moriarty's supposed to be a genius, too.

102
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:20,420
So, I'm Moriarty, and I saw that very thing coming,

103
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:21,780
and therefore I did the opposite.

104
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:23,480
Then why am I here?

105
00:04:23,660 --> 00:04:24,480
Because you saw it.

106
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:26,960
So, anyway, the simple explanation is

107
00:04:26,960 --> 00:04:28,760
that Holmes found Moriarty because he got lucky.

108
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:33,540
And they both were doing 50-50, you know,

109
00:04:33,540 --> 00:04:35,040
a mixed-value Nash equilibrium.

110
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:38,260
But, of course, half the time that will lead to Holmes winning

111
00:04:38,260 --> 00:04:39,840
and the other half the time Moriarty will.

112
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:42,860
And then in each case, the other one could be gloating.

113
